{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,280p' /app/agent-output/S17-external-ui-plugin-onboarding-trap/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"# S17 — External UI plugin onboarding trap\n\n## 1. Failure 1: one bad client bundle disables the whole plugin set\n\nThe `insert` rows are resolved first, but the browser does not receive three\nindependent ES modules. At boot the host reads the installed client files and\nassembles all 53 loader entries into one 4.5 MB **classic-script** combo. The\nentries are expected to be loader records of the form shown by the working\nplugin:\n\n```js\nwindow.__ModuleLoader__.load({\n  id: \"@some/plugin\",\n  factory: (require) => {\n    var module = { exports: {} };\n    var exports = module.exports;\n    // transpiled CommonJS body, using require(...)\n    return module.exports;\n  },\n});\n```\n\nThe new `client.js` begins with a static ESM import and also contains an ESM\n`export`. Those tokens cannot be parsed in the classic combo (`import React`\nis at position 1:1). The combo therefore fails before the loader can complete\nregistration. This is why the stock entries disappear too: registration is\nbeing attempted from one assembled script, and its compile failure aborts the\nwhole load, rather than merely rejecting `dsh-profiles`.\n\n`@deepseek-ai/dsh-typert-registry` is a misleading reporting context, not the\nculprit. The loader error names the entry whose import/await path observed the\nrejected combo. The error's bundle path points to the actual failing source:\n`@lhh010/dsh-profiles/lib/client.js`. The stock package was not modified.\n\n## 2. Diagnosis and the required client format\n\nThe first useful distinction is **compile-time combo failure** versus\n**apply-time plugin failure**. For a compile failure, inspect or bisect the\n*set of client bundles*, not the entry named in the rejection. Disable half of\nthe inserted/installed client entries, restart (because assembly is boot-time),\nand repeat; with this fixture, comparing the baseline with the\n`dsh-profiles` row is the shortest bisect.\n\nA cheap preflight is a static scan of every external client file for\ntop-level `import` or `export` declarations, for example:\n\n```sh\nrg -n '^[[:space:]]*(import|export)[[:space:]]' plugin-root\n```\n\nIt should also verify that the first executable form is the host loader\nwrapper and that the factory returns `module.exports`. A parser check against\nclassic-script grammar is a stronger version of the same test. Dynamic\napplication code and React itself are not the problem; the untransformed ESM\npackaging is.\n\nAn external plugin must ship the host's loader-compatible classic wrapper,\nnot bare ESM. Its build should transform the body to CommonJS-like code inside\n`window.__ModuleLoader__.load({ id, factory })`, create the factory-local\n`module`/`exports`, and return `module.exports`. React and React DOM are host\ndependencies obtained through the injected resolver, for example\n`require(\"react\")`, `require(\"react-dom\")`, or the host-supported\n`require(\"react/jsx-runtime\")`; they should not be bare browser imports (nor\nan incompatible private React copy). The returned exports object must expose\nthe plugin entry point, in particular `apply` (for example, transpiled\n`exports.apply = apply`).\n\n## 3. Failure 2: the slot path is not a declaration\n\nThis second error proves that the bundle now parses and the plugin's `apply`\nfunction is running. `settings.section` is a slot in the host settings\nhierarchy. Slots are declared by the entry that owns the UI hierarchy: the\nparent entry's children table must contain the `section` child. A consumer\ncannot create such a slot merely by passing the dotted path as a top-level\n`name`. A bare registration is rejected when the registry looks up the\ndeclaration and finds no declared child (or the owner has not been declared\nyet).\n\nThe registration must retain the parent/child shape, with `section` nested\nunder its declared parent:\n\n```js\nctx.slots.register(\n  {\n    name: 'settings',\n    children: {\n      section: {\n        id: 'profiles-manager',\n        order: 5,\n      },\n    },\n  },\n  ProfilesSection,\n)\n```\n\nIn other words, the child key represents `settings.section`; it must not be\nflattened to `{ name: 'settings.section', ... }`. The host/settings entry must\nalready declare that child and be applied before this registration.\n\nThe registrant supplies its identity and placement (`id` and `order`) and the\ncomponent supplied as the registration's second argument. The parent owns the\nslot's declaration metadata, including its name/path, `kind` (`section`), and\n`scope` (`settings`); the registrant must not invent or override those fields\nwith the failed call's top-level `name`, `kind`, or `scope`. If the host does\nnot expose `settings.section`, the external plugin cannot fix that by changing\nits own registration—the host must declare a supported slot.\n\n## 4. Dev-loop discipline\n\nThe host assembles and serves the combo once during host boot. It does not\nwatch plugin files and rebuild that combo after an edit. Consequently, neither\na file edit nor a browser hard refresh sees a changed client bundle until the\nhost process is restarted.\n\nThe safe iteration is: stop the existing host and its child processes, verify\nthat its listening port is free, start exactly one host so it assembles a new\ncombo, wait for the boot line, then hard-refresh the browser and check\nSettings → Plugins. An empty plugin list means the combo failed again.\n\nOn Windows, closing the launching terminal can leave the Node child/process\ntree alive. Starting a second host then fails with `EADDRINUSE` because the\norphan still owns the port; that is a process-lifecycle failure, not evidence\nof another plugin bug. Gracefully stop it, or use the documented fallback\n`taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F` to terminate the whole tree, then restart.\n\n## 5. Prevention\n\nHost-side safeguards:\n\n- Validate each source bundle as a classic script, and validate the loader\n  wrapper/returned `apply`, before concatenating the combo. Report the bundle\n  path, plugin id, and line/column of the bad ESM token.\n- Keep an entry-to-byte-range/source-map manifest for the combo, or compile\n  entries independently on failure. Never use the first awaited entry as the\n  presumed source of a combo-wide parse error.\n- At apply time, report the missing slot, its owning parent, the plugin making\n  the registration, and the parent's available declared children. Check the\n  declaration before invoking external code.\n- Optionally quarantine one invalid entry and continue loading known-good\n  entries, while making the quarantined plugin visible in the plugin UI/log.\n\nAn external-plugin template/checklist should include:\n\n1. Build with the host's client-bundle tool: loader `load` wrapper, classic\n   script, factory-local CommonJS `module`/`exports`, shared React dependencies\n   through `require`, and an exported `apply`.\n2. Run a no-top-level-ESM/static-wrapper preflight and a smoke test against the\n   host loader.\n3. Look up the host-owned slot declaration first. Register through the\n   parent/`children` wrapper, pass only registrant fields such as `id` and\n   `order`, and do not claim `name`, `kind`, or `scope`.\n4. Document that the patch `insert` only installs the package; every client\n   edit requires a full host restart, one live process tree, a free port, and a\n   browser hard refresh followed by a non-empty plugin-list check.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
